"Giant Planets, Rings and Moons | VirtualDemos",
"Giant Planets, Rings and Moons",
"<strong>Setups in the demo:</strong> Four giants to scale; Saturn would float; Uranus on its side; Saturn’s rings and the Roche limit; A moon torn into a ring; A rock moon survives; Rings shear; Gaps, shepherds and Mimas; Io, Europa, Ganymede {#}:{#}:{#}; Enceladus and Dione {#}:{#}; Triton goes backward; Io’s tides heat it; Io on a circle.",
"The strip across the top switches between four views: Planets, Rings, Moons and Io. Each can be shown flat or in 3D; drag the 3D view to turn it.",
"<strong>Planets</strong> shows Jupiter, Saturn, Uranus and Neptune side by side at their true sizes, each tipped at its real axial tilt, with Earth for scale if you like. The bars underneath compare the planets’ mean densities with water’s. Click a planet for its numbers.",
"<strong>Rings</strong> looks down on a planet’s rings from above its north pole, to scale. A test moon, a loose pile of {#} boulders held together only by its own gravity, orbits at the distance you set, and its density can be anything from fluffy ice to rock. The orange circle is the Roche limit for that density. Start the clock to see whether the moon holds together; the readouts give the share of the moon still in its largest piece and how many orbits it has made. You can also paint a straight line across the rings and watch it wind up.",
"<strong>Moons</strong> shows a planet’s main moons on their real orbits, starting from where they were at noon on {#} January {#} for Jupiter and Saturn. Each moon is drawn {#} times bigger than it really is and colored by its density. Choose a moon on the card for its distance, period and density.",
"<strong>Io</strong> follows Io on its slightly oval orbit, with a close up of Io stretched by Jupiter’s tide. The card sets how oval the orbit is (its eccentricity \\(e\\)), and the readouts give the tidal heating.",
"Record reading stores the numbers for the view on show. Export to worksheet sends the readings to the matching Data table below (Moons readings to the first table, Planets, Rings and Io readings to the tables named for them); Clear starts a new series, which goes into a new copy of the table.",
"Compare the four giant planets with each other and with Earth, find out why Saturn’s rings are rings and not a moon, and see how three of Jupiter’s moons keep each other in step and heat Io from the inside.",
"<strong>Kepler’s third law for moons.</strong> A moon obeys the same law as a planet going round the Sun: the cube of its distance, \\(a^3\\), divided by the square of its period, \\(P^2\\), is the same for every moon of one planet. With \\(a\\) in the same unit of length for every planet (kilometers, say), that number is bigger for a more massive planet, in proportion to its mass, so moons are how we weigh planets. In this worksheet \\(a\\) is measured in radii of each planet and \\(P\\) in days, so the number is the planet’s mass divided by the cube of its radius, and Analysis {#} turns it back into a mass.",
"<strong>Density</strong> is mass divided by volume. Water is {#} g/cm³, ice about {#} g/cm³ and rock about {#} g/cm³. A planet or moon’s mean density tells us roughly what it is made of: a moon of about {#} g/cm³ is mostly rock, and one of about {#} to {#} g/cm³ is a mix of rock and ice. Jupiter and Saturn, the gas giants, are mostly hydrogen and helium, squeezed tightly near their centers. Uranus and Neptune, the ice giants, have atmospheres rich in hydrogen and helium too, but most of their mass is heavier material inside, thought to be water, ammonia and methane ices and rock in proportions not yet well known.",
"<strong>Tides.</strong> A planet pulls harder on the near side of a moon than on the far side, so it stretches the moon along the line between them. Close enough to the planet, the stretch beats the moon’s own gravity, and a moon held together only by its gravity is pulled apart. The distance where this happens is the <strong>Roche limit</strong>. It is farther out for a fluffier moon, because a less dense moon holds itself together more weakly.",
"<strong>Resonances.</strong> When one moon goes round exactly twice for every orbit of another, the two line up at the same places over and over, and their small tugs add up instead of averaging out. Io, Europa and Ganymede are in a {#}:{#}:{#} resonance. The tugs keep Io’s orbit slightly oval, so its distance from Jupiter and the stretch on it change every orbit. The flexing heats Io the way bending a paper clip back and forth warms it.",
"Saturn has about {#} times Earth’s mass and about {#} times Earth’s volume. Is Saturn’s density more or less than Earth’s? Roughly how many times?",
"Which do you think would be pulled apart farther from a planet, a moon made of rock or one made of ice? Why?",
"If an orbit were a perfect circle, would the stretch on a moon change during its orbit? What would that mean for tidal heating?",
"<strong>Part {#}: weighing Jupiter and Saturn with their moons.</strong> In the Moons view, choose Jupiter and record a reading for each of its four big moons: Io, Europa, Ganymede and Callisto. Send the readings to the worksheet, then clear them. Do the same for Saturn’s six moons from Mimas to Titan, and send them to the worksheet. In each copy of the first table, work out \\(a^3\\) and \\(P^2\\) for each moon.",
"<strong>Part {#}: the four giants.</strong> In the Planets view, record a reading for each of Jupiter, Saturn, Uranus and Neptune, and for Earth. Send the readings to the worksheet, then clear them.",
